/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Yaw rate r is how fast the car turns about a vertical axis through its centre of gravity, measured in rad/s.
   - rdes = vδ / (L + K v²)
   - rdes is the desired yaw rate in rad/s; v is vehicle speed in m/s; δ is road-wheel steering angle in rad; L is wheelbase in m; K is the understeer term in s²/m, so K v² is in m.
   - Large K means the car understeers more, so it needs more steering for the same yaw.
   - Five signals do most of the work.
   - The ESP hydraulic unit has a pump and solenoid valves.
   - An off switch lets the driver disable ESP, for example to rock a car out of snow or run on a dynamometer.
   - In understeer the front tyres lose grip first.
   - In oversteer the rear tyres lose grip first.
   - Engine torque reduction backs up the brakes.
*/
